  "incident": [
   {
    "category": "infrastructure",
    "close_code": "Solved (Permanently)",
    "close_notes": "Reporting API unreachable - RESOLVED. Customers could not reach the Reporting service; the on-call initially suspected the reporting-app release that had shipped the same day and were about to roll it back. Before rolling back we traced the delivery path end to end: DNS, the load balancer and the released build were all healthy, so the deploy was NOT the cause. A maintenance window that same night had run a firewall-hardening pass and a subnet migration; comparing the reporting tier's security groups, network ACLs and routes against its healthy peer services showed the hardening had stripped both the load-balancer ingress and the service's egress to its dependencies, a route to the migrated dependency subnet had been left blackholed, and DNS still pointed at the pre-migration VIPs. We restored each hop to the peer baseline (specific subnets and ports, never a blanket 0.0.0.0/0), repointed DNS to the live VIPs, returned the nodes that had been drained for the window to service, removed the dangling records/pool members the migration left behind, and service recovered. Lesson: verify the reported cause, trace the WHOLE dependency path, restore to the peer baseline with least privilege, and leave no dangling config. The suspected release was left untouched. Closed.",
    "closed_at": "2026-04-18 15:30:00",
    "description": "Reporting API was unreachable; a same-day reporting-app release was suspected. See close notes.",
